Description: logpurge takes an optional phase, aligning the purge to a fixed grid
 slapo-accesslog schedules its purge as a runqueue task whose next run is
 computed as "now + interval" at the moment of dispatch. Three consequences
 follow, and all three are unwanted where several providers replicate to each
 other:
 .
   * each cycle absorbs that cycle's dispatch latency and never gives it back,
     so the purge time walks forward without bound;
   * a purge still running when its next run falls due is rescheduled without
     running, re-phasing the task by a whole interval;
   * a restart re-phases the task to the restart instant.
 .
 Consequently the time at which a given provider purges is neither chosen nor
 stable, and two providers cannot be kept from purging together except by
 repeatedly rewriting their configuration.
 .
 This adds an optional third value to logpurge:
 .
   olcAccessLogPurge: <age> <interval> [<phase>]
 .
 With a phase, the task fires at phase + k*interval counted from the epoch,
 taking the first such instant strictly after now. Because the epoch is itself
 midnight UTC, any interval that divides a day gives midnight-relative
 scheduling; intervals that do not divide a day stay on a uniform grid with no
 discontinuity. The next run is derived from absolute time rather than from the
 moment of dispatch, so latency cannot accumulate, an overrun lands on the next
 grid slot, and a restart returns to the same grid.
 .
 Two values behave exactly as before: without a phase the task is unaligned and
 the arithmetic is untouched. The value is a free-form DirectoryString already
 holding two space-separated tokens, so no schema change is required.
 .
 The alignment is opt-in per runqueue task: the new re_s fields default to zero
 and only slapo-accesslog sets them, leaving syncrepl, back-mdb, back-asyncmeta,
 back-wt, back-ldap, dds, refint, pcache and lloadd on the existing path.
 .
 A task is still inserted due immediately, so configuring a phase purges once
 off-grid and every subsequent run is aligned.
 .
 The runqueue entry's scheduling fields are written under slapd_rq.rq_mutex.
 ldap_pvt_runqueue_resched() reads interval (and now phase and aligned) while
 holding that mutex, and the daemon thread calls it concurrently with a config
 modification. The existing code took the lock only when inserting a new task
 and left the live update of re->interval.tv_sec unlocked; that write is moved
 inside the lock along with the two new ones. A SIGSEGV was observed 13 s after
 a three-value to two-value logpurge change on a server whose purge task had
 been running every 100 s, and not on a server whose purge task had never run.
 .
 log_cf_gen() does not hold rq_mutex on entry: the mutex is a plain
 (non-recursive) pthread mutex, and log_cf_gen() already takes it itself in the
 SLAP_CONFIG_DEL path for LOG_PURGE, which would self-deadlock were it held.
Forwarded: no
Author: Dominik Epple <dominik.epple@gmail.com>
